7592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1045 "General Solutions of Linear ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d59d69ae-798b-5a20-9d5f-60ce55a98799" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153061 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153061 #edb/ref 1061 23719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153061 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d46b30d-17ce-5fe6-a7be-dcb1352a6151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153061 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153062 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153062 #edb/ref 1061 23720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153062 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "70b202ae-ec41-5eae-8ae1-a2c1f7937280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153062 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1061 23721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2968451-7379-5754-b957-4337d8ab3c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153063 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153064 #edb/ref 1061 23722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153064 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15d98898-5aa7-5f1a-9c11-0b4e8416b8d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153064 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153290 #edb/ref 1061 601711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c268f62a-7fd0-540c-9ff9-832b5389a31e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153291 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153291 #edb/ref 1061 601712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f0a0cdaa-70e3-5041-a39e-64dbcbe7a4a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153292 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153292 #edb/ref 1061 601715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153292 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66cf83ce-b3bc-50bd-88ae-2b9f54096657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153292 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153293 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153293 #edb/ref 1061 601718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38e288bc-7c3f-536d-a984-63e199038d6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1061 601721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4499c456-0b30-59ea-92cd-0a6e8cc58d01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153295 #edb/ref 1061 601722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153295 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2779fb81-53c1-5556-969d-409a83b9a273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153295 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1064 "Similar to first-order linear ODEs, for inhomogeneous differential equations, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the **complementary solution** (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a **particular solution** of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWe will apply this structure to a specific differential equation in the next slide." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1065 14780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d323d4d9-40c7-5a00-aafe-a0c1ba46fd4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1067 "General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to evaluate the line integral with respect to arc length\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_Cf(x,y)\\:\\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y),$ where $C$ is a curve in the $xy$-plane defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta),\\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this line integral, we need to recall the following facts:\n\n- A polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta,$ where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\n- The arc length element $\\textrm d s$ in plane polar coordinates is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formulas into our line integral, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's go through an example in which we apply this result." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1065 3987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f970309b-6c25-53f1-a3b5-b094ebe87109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153355 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the second-order linear homogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 5y' + 6y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that a fundamental set of solutions is given by\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe superposition principle tells us that any linear combination of solutions to a linear, homogeneous differential equation is also a solution to the equation. Therefore, the **general solution** to this differential equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{3x} + Be^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are **arbitrary constants.**\n\nIn general, if an $n$th order homogeneous, linear ODE has a fundamental set of solutions given by \n\n$$\ny_1(x), \\quad y_2(x),\\quad \\ldots, \\quad y_n(x),\n$$\n\nthen the general solution to the equation is \n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = C_1y_1(x)+C_2y_2(x)+\\cdots+C_ny_n(x)}\n$$\n\nfor arbitrary constants $C_1, C_2,\\ldots C_n.$\n\n**Note:** Using sigma notation, we can write the general solution more compactly as\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n C_i y_i(x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1065 4197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d0ae06ba-917f-56b1-8d5d-fac54f464ee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157276 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157276 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157276 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157276 #edb/r